# @AERO_PROJECT_NAME@

A C++ game built against the Aero Engine SDK: a window, a lit cube that spins, and a camera and a light to see it by.
It is the hello-cube of the engine's C++ project template. `src/main.cpp` is the whole game.

## Build

The game builds against an installed engine, never against its source tree. In the engine checkout, configure its SDK
preset, build it and install it (`<os>` is `macos`, `windows` or `linux`):

```text
cmake --preset <os>-sdk
cmake --build --preset <os>-sdk
cmake --install build/<os>-sdk
```

Then, from this folder, build the game against that prefix (`<engine>` is the engine checkout):

```text
cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=<engine>/build/sdk/<os>-sdk
cmake --build build --config Release
```

The SDK is built with its own compiler, and this project must be built with that one. A Debug build on Windows is
refused, because the SDK is a Release build.

## Run

```text
./build/@AERO_PROJECT_ID@
```

With Visual Studio's generator the executable is `build\Release\@AERO_PROJECT_ID@.exe`. The build copies the shaders
into a `shaders` folder beside the executable.

`--frames <n>` stops after n frames and prints one line, `hello-cube: frames=<n> updates=<u>`.

| Key | Does |
|---|---|
| Arrow keys | move the cube |
| Escape | quit |

## Your own components

A game registers its own components with `world.registerComponent<T>("name")` before it uses them, as `main.cpp` does
for `Spin`. A component needs no `AERO_COMPONENT` marker: outside the engine's code generator it compiles and does
nothing.
